sabled":false,"content":{"k":"define","target":{"k":"var","name":"d"},"value":{"k":"range","first":{"k":"op","op":"mult","args":[{"k":"real","v":0},{"k":"id","name":"mm"}]},"second":{"k":"op","op":"mult","args":[{"k":"real","v":5},{"k":"id","name":"mm"}]},"last":{"k":"op","op":"mult","args":[{"k":"real","v":300},{"k":"id","name":"mm"}]}},"global":false,"stored":null,"unit":null,"shown":false}},{"id":"37","left":54,"top":1261,"alignX":54,"alignY":1261,"disabled":false,"content":{"k":"plot","x":{"k":"id","name":"d"},"ys":[{"k":"call","fn":{"k":"id","name":"cost"},"args":[{"k":"id","name":"d"}]}],"markers":false,"width":260,"height":150,"xAxis":{"min":null,"max":null,"log":false,"unit":{"k":"id","name":"mm"}},"yAxis":{"min":{"k":"real","v":0},"max":null,"log":false},"traces":[{"color":"#2563eb","weight":2}]}}],"texts":[{"id":"1","left":54,"top":72,"width":440,"height":40.5,"text":"Heat loss through a wall. A house wall of four layers keeps a room at 20 °C on a −5 °C day. How much heat leaks through it, how cold is each layer, and how much would thicker insulation save in a year?"},{"id":"2","left":54,"top":120.5,"width":440,"height":27,"text":"The layers, from inside to out: plasterboard, mineral wool, plywood and brick. Thickness and conductivity of each, as vectors."},{"id":"7","left":54,"top":235.5,"width":440,"height":40.5,"text":"Each layer resists heat in proportion to its thickness over its conductivity; the arrow divides element by element. Thin films of still air on each face add a little more, and the resistances add up in series:"},{"id":"13","left":54,"top":394,"width":440,"height":27,"text":"The heat loss. °C turns a Celsius reading into a temperature to calculate with; a result shows in °C when you choose that unit."},{"id":"21","left":54,"top":523,"width":440,"height":40.5,"text":"Inside the wall. The same heat flow crosses every layer, so the temperature drops in proportion to each one's resistance. Interface j comes after j layers; (i < j) is 1 for the layers before it."},{"id":"28","left":334,"top":894,"width":214,"height":60,"text":"Temperature through the wall, from the room on the left to outdoors on the right. Almost all the drop is across the wool: it does nearly all the work."},{"id":"29","left":54,"top":1058,"width":440,"height":27,"text":"Is more insulation worth it? A heating season of 3000 kelvin-days, with heat at 12 cents a kilowatt-hour. The cost of the wall each year, for wool of thickness d:"},{"id":"38","left":354,"top":1291,"width":194,"height":60,"text":"Dollars a year against wool thickness. The first few centimeters save the most; past about 150 mm each extra one buys little."}],"opaque":[],"origin":0,"tol":0.001,"exactCompare":true,"zeroOverZero":false,"otherRegions":{},"componentOutputs":[]}}